# chip_stack — the ON-GRID STACKED chip variant, TOP-DOWN.
#
# The comparison twin of chip_stack_bottomup.buda: same design, same layer
# policy, SAME TRACK TECHNOLOGY, but no bottom-up templates — every instance's
# interconnect is planned individually in the global frame.  Use the pair to
# separate what the on-grid stacked placement buys from what solve-once-copy
# buys; that only works if both halves route on the same technology, so this
# one sources chip_tracks_mirror.buda too.
#
# It gains nothing from the mirror-symmetric ORIGINS (no templates, so nothing
# to keep in phase); what it does gain is the shared stack's extra tracks —
# 277/3262/161 before the 8 -> 10 track change, 103/2053/125 after.  The
# origins-only part of the pair is the piece it cannot use, which is the point:
# symmetry earns its keep exactly where solve-once-copy is in play.
#
# Design (see chip_stack_bottomup.buda for the full note on the 504 grid):
#   LEFT  column  x=[0..6100]      2 x big2, pitch 7560  (multiple of 504)
#   RIGHT column  x=[7100..9430]   4 x mix2, pitch 3528  (multiple of 504),
#                                  centred (458 margins); upper half mirrored
#   die 9430 x 13860, 100 top-level cross-hierarchy buses, 11750 nets
source chip_tracks_mirror.buda
open_bdb chip_stack.bdb.sql
reserve_top_layers 2
derive_busterms 2
add_blocks_from_bdb 0
add_blocks_from_bdb 1 skip
add_blocks_from_bdb 2 skip
set_max_bundle_bits auto
run_hier_bundler depth 2
generate_hier_topologies
set_planner_param healersAhead 1
run_planner hier 5 signal_tracks
run_nuts
run_detailed_nuts
negotiate_congestion
report_wl
check_design
exit
